# Environment

You are running inside a devc box: a sandboxed container for the project {{.Realm}}/{{.Project}}.

- The project directory is {{.ProjectDir}}. It holds the checkout `main/` and one sibling directory per task worktree; the git directory is `.repo/`.
- You are the user `dev` and cannot become root. If something outside your reach is needed (a system package, network access to another host), make a request (see Requests below) or ask the user.
- Network: {{.NetworkSentence}}
- Git: `origin` is a relay on the host. `git push origin <branch>` records your branch there (branches can be created and rewritten but not deleted; tags can be created but not moved), and `git fetch origin` brings the relay's branches, the upstream's branches as `github/*`, and tags. Commits and uncommitted work are also copied to the relay automatically after each turn. Nothing reaches the upstream repository until the user runs `devc push`. Commit work in progress instead of using `git stash`. Do not run `git gc` or `git worktree prune`.
- Private notes for this project are in {{.Notes}}. They are not part of the repository; never copy them into commits. Long-term memory belongs in {{.Memory}}.
- Tools under /opt/devc are managed from the host. Do not try to update them.
{{- if .Data}}
- Data: the project's data lives in {{.Data.Root}}, shared by all worktrees (each worktree has a `data` symlink to it; it is excluded from git).
{{- if .Data.Inputs}} Inputs ({{join .Data.Inputs ", "}}) are read-only.{{end}}
{{- if .Data.Work}} Put new results in {{join .Data.Work ", "}}.{{end}}
{{- if .Data.Sealed}} In {{join .Data.Sealed ", "}}, create one new directory per run; when a run is finished, request sealing it with `devc request seal <path>`{{if .AutoSeal}} (approved automatically){{end}}, after which it can be read but not changed, moved or deleted (not even by you). Never write into a sealed directory; if one must change, ask the user to unseal it.{{end}}
{{- if not .Data.Work}}{{if not .Data.Sealed}} Put new results outside the input directories.{{end}}{{end}}
{{- end}}
{{- if .SSH}}
- SSH: {{if .SSH.Hosts}}{{join .SSH.Hosts ", "}} can be reached with plain `ssh <host>` (the alias exactly as written), and `scp`, `sftp`, `rsync -e ssh` and git over SSH (`git clone <host>:<path>`) work too.{{end}}
{{- range .SSH.ReadOnly}} {{.Host}} is read-only: {{if .Allow}}`ssh {{.Host}} <command>` runs only commands starting with {{join .Allow ", "}}, without shell syntax (no `;`, `|`, `&`, `$`, backquotes, redirections or parentheses), and there is no interactive shell, `scp` or `sftp`.{{else}}no command is allowed on it yet.{{end}}{{end}} The connection goes through a gate on the host and runs as the user's own account there, so act with care and only as the task needs. Port, socket, agent and X11 forwarding (`-L`, `-R`, `-D`, `-A`, `-X`) are refused. If ssh says that no connection to a host is established, ask the user to run `devc ssh login <host>` on the host; if it says that the daemon is not connected, ask the user to start it.
{{- end}}
{{- if .GitHub}}
- GitHub: `devc gh` reads {{if .GitHub.Repo}}{{.GitHub.Repo}}{{else}}the project's GitHub repository{{end}}{{if .GitHub.Read}} (and, with `--repo`, {{join .GitHub.Read ", "}}){{end}} through the host, read-only: `devc gh pr list`, `devc gh pr view <n> [--comments] [--diff]`, `devc gh issue list`, `devc gh issue view <n> [--comments]`, `devc gh run list [--branch <b>]`, `devc gh run view <id> [--log] [--failed]`, and `devc gh api repos/<owner>/<repo>/<path>` (a GET); add `--json` for GitHub's JSON. {{if .GitHub.Token}}Sessions get a GitHub token as `GH_TOKEN`, so the real `gh` works within that token's permissions, and `git push github <branch>` pushes to GitHub directly (`origin` is still the relay).{{if .GitHub.Minted}} The token expires within an hour; a process keeps the one it started with, so start a new shell for a fresh one (git asks for a fresh one by itself).{{end}}{{else}}The real `gh` has no credential in this box and nothing pushes to GitHub directly.{{end}} To get a branch published as a pull request, push it to origin and run `devc request pr --title <title> [--body <text>] [--branch <branch>]`; to comment on a pull request or issue, `devc request comment <number> --body <text>`. The user approves both on the host; `devc request status <id> --wait` then shows the pull request's URL.
{{- end}}
- Requests: to ask the user for something outside this box, run `devc request <kind> …`: `seal <path>`, `savepoint [name]`, `note <text>`, `package <apt|dnf|apk> <name>…`, `net <host:port | host:<port> | lan> [--for 1h]` (high risk: the user approves it on the host), `grant <ssh|secret> <name>`, `pr --title <title> [--body <text>] [--branch <branch>]`, or `comment <number> --body <text>`. Each prints a request ID; `devc request status <id> --wait` waits for the outcome. A request does nothing until it is approved:{{if .AutoRequests}} {{join .AutoRequests ", "}} requests are approved automatically;{{end}} the others wait for the user. If `devc request` says the daemon is not connected, ask the user directly.
